1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,568
A chimpanzee has 48 chromosomes.

2
00:00:03,635 --> 00:00:05,627
You have 46.

3
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:08,264
So where did the other two go?

4
00:00:08,769 --> 00:00:13,240
Your brain already has an answer ready:
we came from monkeys.

5
00:00:13,307 --> 00:00:18,531
And right behind it, another question.
So why are there still monkeys?

6
00:00:18,605 --> 00:00:21,384
Both questions lead to the same answer.

7
00:00:21,451 --> 00:00:24,922
And it's hidden inside you,
in three clues.

8
00:00:24,989 --> 00:00:28,520
The last one is part of the reason
you were born at all.

9
00:00:28,741 --> 00:00:30,520
First, the myth.

10
00:00:30,587 --> 00:00:33,597
We didn't come from any monkey
alive today.

11
00:00:33,664 --> 00:00:35,810
Not even Darwin said that.

12
00:00:35,888 --> 00:00:39,437
Chimps aren't our grandparents.
They're our cousins.

13
00:00:39,504 --> 00:00:41,958
And cousins share a grandparent.

14
00:00:42,119 --> 00:00:45,881
Ours lived roughly
6 to 8 million years ago.

15
00:00:45,962 --> 00:00:50,510
Since then, their branch has been
evolving exactly as long as ours.

16
00:00:50,577 --> 00:00:52,356
Just in a different direction.

17
00:00:52,423 --> 00:00:55,339
There's no such thing as more evolved.

18
00:00:55,423 --> 00:00:58,569
Evolution is a tree, not a ladder.

19
00:00:58,647 --> 00:01:01,888
And that grandparent
had grandparents of its own.

20
00:01:01,955 --> 00:01:04,734
But today, we're following one branch.

21
00:01:04,801 --> 00:01:05,870
Yours.

22
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:07,267
That's the claim.

23
00:01:07,334 --> 00:01:11,652
But if it's true,
two chromosomes can't just disappear.

24
00:01:11,719 --> 00:01:14,998
They have to be somewhere.
So let's find them.

25
00:01:15,065 --> 00:01:17,173
Your body kept the evidence.

26
00:01:17,301 --> 00:01:21,734
Every cell in your body
carries about two meters of DNA.

27
00:01:21,801 --> 00:01:24,217
Your complete instruction manual.

28
00:01:24,297 --> 00:01:28,615
To fit inside a cell,
it's packed into separate volumes.

29
00:01:28,682 --> 00:01:31,212
Those volumes are chromosomes.

30
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:33,377
And you have two sets of them.

31
00:01:33,444 --> 00:01:36,300
One from your mother,
one from your father.

32
00:01:36,367 --> 00:01:39,300
A chimp's set has 24 volumes.

33
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:43,300
Two sets make 48. Yours has 23.

34
00:01:43,367 --> 00:01:45,667
Two sets make 46.

35
00:01:45,743 --> 00:01:49,581
So in each set,
just one volume is missing.

36
00:01:49,743 --> 00:01:51,120
Where did it go?

37
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:54,059
And you can't just rip out a volume.

38
00:01:54,143 --> 00:01:56,905
Each one holds hundreds of instructions.

39
00:01:57,066 --> 00:01:59,905
Lose one, and you don't survive.

40
00:01:59,987 --> 00:02:03,150
So one real possibility is left.

41
00:02:03,217 --> 00:02:06,440
Two volumes were bound together into one.

42
00:02:06,512 --> 00:02:10,522
And gorillas? Orangutans? 48 as well.

43
00:02:10,589 --> 00:02:13,889
So this happened only on our branch.

44
00:02:13,966 --> 00:02:17,881
But that's still just a claim.
How would you prove it?

45
00:02:18,209 --> 00:02:23,509
In 1982, scientists lined up
human and chimp chromosomes.

46
00:02:23,747 --> 00:02:28,740
Stained under a microscope,
each one shows stripes, like a barcode.

47
00:02:28,911 --> 00:02:35,058
Our chromosome two matched
two chimp chromosomes, laid end to end.

48
00:02:35,296 --> 00:02:37,135
Stripe for stripe.

49
00:02:37,209 --> 00:02:40,988
Impressive.
But stripes can match by chance.

50
00:02:41,055 --> 00:02:43,586
They needed something you can't fake.

51
00:02:43,715 --> 00:02:47,033
Every chromosome
ends with the same short code,

52
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,187
repeated over and over.

53
00:02:49,254 --> 00:02:53,631
Like the plastic tip on a shoelace,
the one that keeps it from fraying.

54
00:02:53,708 --> 00:02:57,718
So if two chromosomes were joined
end to end, there should

55
00:02:57,785 --> 00:03:02,718
be two of those tips facing each other,
right in the middle of chromosome two.

56
00:03:02,785 --> 00:03:07,238
And if they weren't there,
the whole idea was in serious trouble.

57
00:03:07,549 --> 00:03:11,195
In 1991, they found it.

58
00:03:11,318 --> 00:03:14,003
Right in the middle of chromosome two.

59
00:03:14,395 --> 00:03:16,772
Two endings, back to back.

60
00:03:17,107 --> 00:03:19,347
Then they found something else.

61
00:03:19,414 --> 00:03:22,886
Every chromosome
has exactly one pinch point,

62
00:03:22,953 --> 00:03:25,637
where it's pulled apart
when a cell divides.

63
00:03:25,799 --> 00:03:30,560
Chromosome two has two.
One of them, switched off.

64
00:03:30,639 --> 00:03:33,631
Two endings. Two pinch points.

65
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:36,785
One chromosome. That's the scar.

66
00:03:37,309 --> 00:03:41,934
But that's only half the mystery.
If it fused, how?

67
00:03:42,001 --> 00:03:47,763
And how did something that happened inside
one person reach eight billion people?

68
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:51,616
In an egg or a sperm
cell of one of our ancestors,

69
00:03:51,683 --> 00:03:55,000
two chromosomes stuck together,
end to end.

70
00:03:55,067 --> 00:03:58,769
No instructions were lost.
That child was healthy.

71
00:03:58,836 --> 00:04:01,521
It just had 47 chromosomes.

72
00:04:01,597 --> 00:04:03,376
And it still happens.

73
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:07,684
About one person in a thousand
carries a fused chromosome.

74
00:04:07,751 --> 00:04:09,974
Most of them never find out.

75
00:04:10,053 --> 00:04:11,815
Here's a living example.

76
00:04:11,977 --> 00:04:17,738
Przewalski's horse, the wild
horse of Mongolia, has 66 chromosomes.

77
00:04:17,823 --> 00:04:20,277
A domestic horse has 64.

78
00:04:20,438 --> 00:04:23,063
Yet together, they have healthy foals.

79
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:25,969
And those foals have foals of their own.

80
00:04:26,047 --> 00:04:29,211
When two people with 47 had children,

81
00:04:29,278 --> 00:04:33,365
some of those children got the fused
chromosome from both sides.

82
00:04:33,432 --> 00:04:37,211
From the mother, and from the father. 46.

83
00:04:37,278 --> 00:04:40,270
That's why two are missing, not one.

84
00:04:40,493 --> 00:04:44,811
In small groups of early humans,
that version probably spread,

85
00:04:44,878 --> 00:04:49,101
generation after generation,
until all of us had it.

86
00:04:49,179 --> 00:04:52,381
And when? The DNA of the Denisovans,

87
00:04:52,448 --> 00:04:56,633
our extinct cousins,
carries the same fused chromosome.

88
00:04:56,794 --> 00:05:00,266
So the fusion is older
than our split from them.

89
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:04,787
One recent estimate:
about 900,000 years ago.

90
00:05:05,115 --> 00:05:06,030
But wait.

91
00:05:06,268 --> 00:05:10,048
One fusion on its own doesn't
prove we're related to chimps.

92
00:05:10,115 --> 00:05:14,201
It could be a coincidence.
So we need two more clues.

93
00:05:14,268 --> 00:05:16,971
And the third is the one I promised you.

94
00:05:17,038 --> 00:05:19,722
The reason you were born at all.

95
00:05:20,149 --> 00:05:22,833
Clue two starts at sea.

96
00:05:22,913 --> 00:05:28,923
On long voyages, sailors' gums bled,
their teeth fell out, and they died.

97
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:33,752
Historians estimate scurvy
killed around two million sailors.

98
00:05:33,830 --> 00:05:38,301
In 1747, a ship's surgeon named James Lind

99
00:05:38,368 --> 00:05:43,438
split twelve sick sailors into pairs,
and gave each pair something different.

100
00:05:43,676 --> 00:05:47,745
The two who got oranges
and lemons recovered fastest.

101
00:05:47,825 --> 00:05:51,509
But the ship's rats were fine.
So was the ship's dog.

102
00:05:51,671 --> 00:05:53,663
Why? Take a guess.

103
00:05:54,291 --> 00:05:57,377
Vitamin C. Rats make their own.

104
00:05:57,444 --> 00:06:00,437
Dogs make their own. Most mammals do.

105
00:06:00,515 --> 00:06:01,585
We can't.

106
00:06:01,746 --> 00:06:05,141
We still have the instructions for it,
but they're broken.

107
00:06:05,208 --> 00:06:08,738
That's why you need oranges,
and your dog doesn't.

108
00:06:08,810 --> 00:06:11,743
And chimps, gorillas and orangutans?

109
00:06:11,810 --> 00:06:13,187
Broken too.

110
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:14,734
Coincidence?

111
00:06:14,801 --> 00:06:17,871
Maybe it broke in each of us, separately.

112
00:06:18,097 --> 00:06:22,337
Here's the test.
Guinea pigs can't make vitamin C either.

113
00:06:22,404 --> 00:06:26,627
Their gene broke too,
but in a completely different way.

114
00:06:26,705 --> 00:06:31,697
Ours and the apes' share the same damage,
in the same places.

115
00:06:31,774 --> 00:06:36,246
It's how a teacher spots two essays
copied from the same source.

116
00:06:36,313 --> 00:06:40,074
Not the right answers.
The identical mistakes.

117
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,646
And clue three is the strangest.

118
00:06:43,882 --> 00:06:48,490
About 8% of your DNA
wasn't written by your ancestors.

119
00:06:48,651 --> 00:06:51,720
It came from viruses that infected them.

120
00:06:51,798 --> 00:06:56,192
Some viruses paste their own code
into the cells they infect.

121
00:06:56,259 --> 00:07:02,477
If they hit an egg or a sperm, that code
gets passed down, for millions of years.

122
00:07:02,544 --> 00:07:04,938
They can land almost anywhere.

123
00:07:05,005 --> 00:07:10,074
Yet many of ours sit in exactly
the same spots in chimp DNA.

124
00:07:10,313 --> 00:07:14,151
Same scribbles. Same page. Again.

125
00:07:14,379 --> 00:07:17,909
But one of those scribbles
wasn't useless at all.

126
00:07:18,035 --> 00:07:21,122
Its code makes a protein called syncytin.

127
00:07:21,189 --> 00:07:23,968
And syncytin helps build the placenta,

128
00:07:24,035 --> 00:07:27,489
the organ that kept you alive
before you were born.

129
00:07:27,681 --> 00:07:33,230
So part of the reason you were born
is a virus that infected our ancestors

130
00:07:33,297 --> 00:07:36,443
more than 25 million years ago.

131
00:07:36,869 --> 00:07:40,726
And yes. Chimps carry it too. Same virus.

132
00:07:40,793 --> 00:07:42,169
Same spot.

133
00:07:42,696 --> 00:07:46,534
Three clues, all written in DNA.

134
00:07:46,696 --> 00:07:49,437
But a good detective
wants a second witness,

135
00:07:49,504 --> 00:07:51,842
from somewhere else entirely.

136
00:07:51,973 --> 00:07:57,906
DNA says our branch split from the chimps'
roughly 6 to 8 million years ago.

137
00:07:57,973 --> 00:07:59,137
If that's true,

138
00:07:59,204 --> 00:08:03,675
the oldest members of our family
should turn up in rocks about that old.

139
00:08:03,742 --> 00:08:09,119
Darwin even guessed where: Africa,
where chimps and gorillas live today.

140
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:12,387
In 2001, in the desert of Chad,

141
00:08:12,454 --> 00:08:16,369
a team found a skull
about 7 million years old.

142
00:08:16,531 --> 00:08:18,848
A brain the size of a chimp's.

143
00:08:18,915 --> 00:08:22,677
But small canine teeth,
and a flatter face.

144
00:08:22,806 --> 00:08:28,046
In Ethiopia: Ardi, 4.4 million years old.

145
00:08:28,113 --> 00:08:30,431
Feet that could still grip branches,

146
00:08:30,498 --> 00:08:33,644
and hips already
shaped for walking upright.

147
00:08:33,772 --> 00:08:37,474
Lucy, 3.2 million years old.

148
00:08:37,541 --> 00:08:40,166
A brain not much bigger than a chimp's.

149
00:08:40,233 --> 00:08:43,302
But her hips and legs walked like ours.

150
00:08:43,428 --> 00:08:47,515
And in Tanzania,
about 3.6 million years ago,

151
00:08:47,582 --> 00:08:52,036
a small group of her kind
walked across fresh volcanic ash.

152
00:08:52,198 --> 00:08:54,421
Their footprints are still there.

153
00:08:54,751 --> 00:08:59,838
Two witnesses that never met.
One reads DNA in a lab.

154
00:08:59,905 --> 00:09:04,282
The other digs in rock.
And they tell the same story.

155
00:09:04,709 --> 00:09:07,778
You might be wondering
what this means for faith.

156
00:09:08,094 --> 00:09:10,950
DNA shows how bodies changed.

157
00:09:11,017 --> 00:09:14,701
It doesn't say whether life has a meaning,
or a maker.

158
00:09:15,026 --> 00:09:18,497
A fused chromosome. A broken gene.

159
00:09:18,564 --> 00:09:19,959
A virus.

160
00:09:20,026 --> 00:09:24,557
Your body is a family history,
written in scars.

161
00:09:24,787 --> 00:09:28,626
Chimps are your cousins.
Not your grandparents.

162
00:09:29,030 --> 00:09:33,040
And those two missing chromosomes?
They were never lost.

163
00:09:33,107 --> 00:09:36,483
They're inside you right now,
holding hands.

164
00:09:37,108 --> 00:09:38,541
Which clue got you?

165
00:09:38,608 --> 00:09:42,041
The scar, the broken gene, or the virus?

166
00:09:42,108 --> 00:09:43,408
Tell us below.

167
00:09:43,837 --> 00:09:49,155
By one estimate,
that fusion is about 900,000 years old.

168
00:09:49,222 --> 00:09:55,291
And by 300,000 years ago, at least eight
kinds of humans were walking the Earth.

169
00:09:55,465 --> 00:09:59,398
Today, only one is left. Why us?

170
00:09:59,465 --> 00:10:02,842
That's the story of our next film.
It's right here.

171
00:10:03,367 --> 00:10:07,897
Odyssey Beyond. Curiosity has no limits.

